            <ArticleTitle>Ileocolic invagination in adult due to caecal carcinoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>190-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In adult invagination, the leading point is usually an organic lesion. It is mostly a benign lesion in enteric, ileocolic and ileocaecal invaginations, however in colocolic invaginations the leading point is often malign. In our patient we diagnosed an ileocolic invagination due to a caecal tumour, evidenced by CT (Computerized tomography) and colonoscopy. Right hemicolectomy and ileotransversostomy were performed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
